From a7c7a8d58bc0cc2b300a7e22cdffde372c0a7fed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Hsu <Daniel-Hsu@quantatw.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 10:17:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] common: fix missing power-gated sensors after BMC reset

[Background & Problem]
The power, POST, and chassis states are cached in global variables
defaulting to false. On daemon startup, setupPowerMatchCallback() issues
async D-Bus queries to fetch the initial states, while the first sensor
scan (createSensors) runs concurrently.

A race condition occurs if the first scan completes before the D-Bus
replies arrive: sensors gated by PowerState ("On", "BiosPost",
"ChassisOn") are skipped due to the stale default values.

Normally, a PropertiesChanged signal recovers this by triggering a
rescan. However, during a BMC-only reset with the host already powered
on, the state never transitions, so no signal is emitted. Since the
initial queries silently updated the cached globals without notifying
the daemon, these sensors remained missing forever.

[Solution]
Pass hostStatusCallback into the initial state queries. If the queried
state differs from the default false, invoke the callback to force the
daemon to rerun createSensors and instantiate the skipped sensors.

[Impact]
- Cold boot behavior is unaffected: queried states match defaults,
  triggering no extra rescans.
- The triggered rescan uses the existing, idempotent PropertiesChanged
  path, which safely reuses existing sensor and device allocations.

Tested:
- Yosemite5: Performed 5000 BMC reset cycles with the host already on.
  Verified no sensors were missing.

PR: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/93385

Change-Id: If3155572ba29928a00c48bd7db7e83186854ae57
Signed-off-by: Daniel Hsu <Daniel-Hsu@quantatw.com>
